title: Copying everything makes copies drift; sharing everything below the first layer makes changes silent. skg chose silence, and deep reporting is its price.
pid: d938625a-4c60-4673-b7f3-db1720af167d
body: |2-
  A federated wiki fork copies the whole page, so each copy goes its own
  way. In Bell's tale four people fork one page in turn over three days,
  and the newest version lacks two of their additions, made on a branch
  its author never saw. Caulfield calls it the "catch-up problem": once
  copies drift, putting them back together while keeping attribution is
  hard. In skg a fork copies one layer and contains the very nodes its
  original does, so an addition below the first layer exists once and
  every follower holds it. Only the first layer can drift, and that is
  the layer a subscription reports. So deep forking, option 2 of
  [[id:d3ed5d37-2a37-441a-b82b-03a39a89fdf2][reporting changes at any depth]], would buy back the
  drift federated wiki suffered: deep reporting should be notices, not copies.
